  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.LocalPath">Local path</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Mode.OnDemand">Download on demand (placeholders)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Mode.Always">Keep fully local</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.MinCopies">Placeholder threshold</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.Thumbs">Create thumbnails on demand</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.CopiesHint">When the space is freed, the local content is gone and the file becomes a placeholder again. This happens only once this many other nodes carry the file completely. With a single server, 1 is right, with several nodes 2. Less than 1 is not allowed, because the last content in the network could then disappear.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.TreeQuestion">What should be shown?</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.SaveHint">Changes take effect when the share is restarted.</s:String>

  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.Title">Connect share</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.LocalFolder">Local folder</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.PathHint">This is the path where the share appears in Explorer. The directory is created if it does not exist yet.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.ModeQuestion">How should the share be kept?</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.TreeQuestion">What should be included?</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.Hint">Branches you leave unchecked are not created. They use no disk space and are not listed in the index.</s:String>

  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Title">Settings</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.CacheGroup">CACHE</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.CacheDir">Cache directory</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.CacheDirHint">This directory holds the shares themselves. Windows fills the placeholders in place; when the space is freed again, the placeholder remains. Not everything in here is cache: pinned files and shares in “keep fully local” mode are kept. New shares are created here, ones already connected stay where they are.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Budget">Cache budget (GB)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.MinFree">Minimum free space (GB)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.LimitsHint">Two limits apply; whichever is reached first takes effect. When a limit is exceeded, the space of the files that went unopened longest is freed — across all shares. Pinned files and shares in “keep fully local” mode are kept. For free space, 0 means no limit.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.ClearCache">Clear cache now …</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.DataGroup">DATA</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.DataDir">Data directory</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.DataDirHint">This directory holds the device certificate and the index; the thumbnails are stored in the cache directory. A change takes effect at the next start. Existing data is not moved, and if there is no certificate at the new location, a new device ID is created.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.NetGroup">CONNECTIONS</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Listen">Accept incoming connections from other devices</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.ListenHint">Without this, SyncTClient only opens connections itself. A remote device that has just added this device cannot reach it, and the “wants to connect” prompt does not appear. Unknown devices are shown to you before a connection is made.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.ListenPort">Port for incoming connections</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Parallelism">Parallel block requests</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.LocalDiscovery">Discover and be discoverable on the local network</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.LocalDiscoveryHint">An announcement is sent to the local network every 30 seconds (UDP 21027), and announcements from other devices are received. Without this, the device cannot be found on the local network: a remote device whose address reads “dynamic” will not find it and will not connect.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Announce">Tell the discovery server where this device can be reached</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.AnnounceHint">The same for connections outside the local network, signed with the device certificate. The server learns this connection’s public IP address in the process.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Lookup">Ask the discovery server for remote devices without a fixed address</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.LookupHint">Needed for a remote device with no port open to the outside: it has no fixed address and instead tells the server how it can be reached. The server learns which device is being requested, and your public IP address as well.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Servers">Discovery servers (one per line)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.ServersHint">The role is part of the address: “nolookup” only accepts announcements, “noannounce” only answers queries. For announcements there is one server each for IPv4 and IPv6. Which address a server sees depends on the protocol used to reach it.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Relays">Use relays when there is no direct route</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.RelaysHint">Master switch. Relays can also be turned off per remote device, under Remote devices → Edit. A relay routes the connection through someone else’s machine. The connection stays encrypted, but it becomes slower.</s:String>

